1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,768
The school assigns burns and hypothermia this week. Its listed
provincial pages also include electrical contact and heat illness, so
these are part of the reading rather than optional missing pages.

2
00:00:12,768 --> 00:00:26,448
A thermal burn needs cooling of the injury while preventing whole-body
heat loss. A hypothermic patient needs protection and the appropriate
rewarming plan. A heat-stroke patient needs rapid active cooling.

3
00:00:26,448 --> 00:00:37,368
Do not combine these into a single temperature checklist. Identify the
mechanism and current physiology, then predict the effect and possible
harm of each intervention.

4
00:00:37,368 --> 00:00:49,896
The paired guides include every named Week Four BCEHS identifier and
all thirteen BCPEG pages, with current-versus-school differences
labelled. They are not a substitute for supervised device competence.

5
00:00:49,896 --> 00:01:00,264
A forty-eight-year-old walks toward the crew after hot liquid and a
brief flame exposure. The right arm and anterior trunk are painful,
and the patient is shivering.

6
00:01:00,264 --> 00:01:10,632
There was also brief smoke exposure in an enclosed space and the voice
is changing. Talking now does not guarantee that the airway will
remain safe during transfer.

7
00:01:10,632 --> 00:01:22,176
Begin scene safety and the primary assessment, stop any continuing
injury and assess for other trauma. The burn appearance must not
distract from breathing or circulation.

8
00:01:22,176 --> 00:01:32,760
Ask the injury time and what cooling has already been effective. That
history informs further care; it is not a reason to restart the clock
and cool indefinitely.

9
00:01:32,760 --> 00:01:45,384
A fire being out does not prove that the air, structure or electrical
system is safe. Use the rescue and hazardous-zone plan rather than
entering because you possess gloves or an oxygen cylinder.

10
00:01:45,384 --> 00:01:58,392
Stop continuing heat exposure and extinguish burning material through
the safe appropriate method. Remove nonadherent clothing and jewellery
that retains heat or may become constrictive as swelling develops.

11
00:01:58,392 --> 00:02:08,040
Do not pull melted clothing or adherent material off the wound.
Protect the area and communicate what remains so the receiving team
can manage it safely.

12
00:02:08,040 --> 00:02:18,720
Chemical and electrical sources need their own hazard control. A
thermal-cooling routine does not replace power isolation or agent-
appropriate decontamination.

13
00:02:18,720 --> 00:02:30,576
Superficial epidermal injury is commonly red, dry and blanching.
Partial-thickness injury extends into dermis and is often moist,
blistered and painful.

14
00:02:30,576 --> 00:02:45,048
Deeper injury can be paler, drier, less blanching and less sensitive.
Full thickness may be leathery, white, brown or charred, with an
insensate centre and painful surrounding mixed injury.

15
00:02:45,048 --> 00:02:57,384
Do not call a painless burn minor or rely on one feature to determine
final depth. Describe the findings and the uncertainty; depth can
evolve after the field examination.

16
00:02:57,384 --> 00:03:08,232
The immediate task is to recognize the significant injury and protect
the patient. Do not delay essential care for a debate over the exact
depth of every centimetre.

17
00:03:08,232 --> 00:03:20,928
The adult rule of nines gives head and neck nine percent, each entire
arm nine, each entire leg eighteen, the anterior trunk eighteen,
posterior trunk eighteen and perineum one.

18
00:03:20,928 --> 00:03:32,712
Those are whole regions. The front of an arm is not the entire arm,
and two sides must not be accidentally counted twice. Estimate the
burned fraction of each region.

19
00:03:32,712 --> 00:03:43,560
For significant burn TBSA, include partial-thickness and deeper
injury, not uncomplicated superficial redness. State which depths were
counted.

20
00:03:43,560 --> 00:03:55,176
Area is an estimate used with location, depth, age, inhalation and
associated injury. It is neither a complete severity score nor an
automatic fluid order.

21
00:03:55,176 --> 00:04:04,920
A child is not an adult scaled down. The relative head and leg areas
change with age, so adult nines can produce a misleading estimate.

22
00:04:04,920 --> 00:04:15,792
The viewed local pediatric burn chart has separate age columns and a
front and back map. Select the correct column and count the actual
affected regions and depths.

23
00:04:15,792 --> 00:04:28,680
Do not take one region from a Lund-Browder-style chart and another
from adult nines without reconciling their definitions. A hand region
on a full chart is not the same as a one-percent palmar comparison.

24
00:04:28,680 --> 00:04:39,048
Communicate the method and uncertainty. An honest approximate area is
more useful than a precise-looking number assembled from incompatible
denominators.

25
00:04:39,048 --> 00:04:49,008
For scattered small areas, the explicit reviewed palmar image uses the
patient palm plus fingers as approximately one percent of total
surface area.

26
00:04:49,008 --> 00:04:58,608
Use the patient hand reference, not the clinician hand. Small patches
can then be compared without assuming every irregular wound is one
complete hand.

27
00:04:58,608 --> 00:05:10,992
The supplied Nancy text says palm excluding fingers equals one
percent. That differs from the viewed explicit reference; do not
silently treat the two as interchangeable.

28
00:05:10,992 --> 00:05:19,992
State the method in the record and handover. A percentage is only
interpretable when the receiver knows what surface and depth you
included.

29
00:05:19,992 --> 00:05:31,536
In our original adult example, the whole anterior trunk has partial-
thickness injury, contributing eighteen percent. The entire right arm
is also partial thickness, contributing nine.

30
00:05:31,536 --> 00:05:40,416
The left arm is red but has only uncomplicated superficial injury. It
is not added to the significant-burn area in this calculation.

31
00:05:40,416 --> 00:05:51,864
The included estimate is twenty-seven percent, not thirty-six. If only
part of a listed region were burned, you would use that fraction
rather than automatically the whole region.

32
00:05:51,864 --> 00:06:03,720
This is a teaching estimate, not an infusion prescription. The patient
still needs airway assessment, source control, appropriate cooling,
temperature protection and a destination plan.

33
00:06:03,720 --> 00:06:14,712
Return to the patient whose voice is changing. Reassess the mouth,
voice, secretions, breathing and mental state while maintaining the
primary-survey priorities.

34
00:06:14,712 --> 00:06:29,112
Hoarseness, soot, stridor or swelling can indicate an evolving
problem. None by itself is an automatic independent intubation order,
and the absence of a dramatic sign does not guarantee future safety.

35
00:06:29,112 --> 00:06:41,760
Request the appropriate advanced airway team early when risk is
significant. State how the airway is changing and what support is
currently effective rather than waiting until access is impossible.

36
00:06:41,760 --> 00:06:55,896
Protect oxygenation and ventilation within scope and reassess
repeatedly. Do not delay effective support to prepare an unverified
nebulized-humidity technique or copy an old airway category literally.

37
00:06:55,896 --> 00:07:08,496
Ask about the enclosed space, exposure duration, loss of
consciousness, symptoms and other people affected. Toxic exposure can
coexist with thermal airway or skin injury.

38
00:07:08,496 --> 00:07:20,952
Conventional pulse oximetry can appear normal with carbon monoxide
exposure. Point-of-care CO-oximetry also has limitations and is not a
dependable stand-alone exclusion test.

39
00:07:20,952 --> 00:07:30,504
Other combustion toxins may contribute. Do not sniff for a remembered
cyanide smell or use the absence of cherry-red skin as reassurance.

40
00:07:30,504 --> 00:07:43,008
Use the indicated oxygen and consultation/conveyance pathway and
communicate the exposure history. The patient condition and exposure
matter more than a single normal display.

41
00:07:43,008 --> 00:07:56,400
Protect the crew and establish a safe treatment area before contact.
Remove contaminated clothing and manage runoff so that neither another
body region nor a rescuer receives secondary exposure.

42
00:07:56,400 --> 00:08:10,512
Safely remove suitable dry material before irrigation and identify the
agent when possible. Do not attempt household acid-base neutralization
or assume every water-reactive substance can be treated identically.

43
00:08:10,512 --> 00:08:27,960
Hydrofluoric acid can have systemic effects despite a small-looking
wound. Do not follow the supplied textbook calcium-chloride-gel
recipe; the reviewed CDC skin guidance explicitly excludes it. Obtain
the appropriate specialist/authorized pathway.

44
00:08:27,960 --> 00:08:39,792
For chemical eye exposure, begin appropriate prompt irrigation without
delaying for a complete visual examination, protect the other eye and
continue reassessment and specialist care.

45
00:08:39,792 --> 00:08:52,656
Do not approach a downed wire or attached patient until qualified
personnel confirm isolation. A line that appears inactive can re-
energize; improvised attempts to move it can create another victim.

46
00:08:52,656 --> 00:09:06,168
Once safe, assess arrest, breathing and circulation and consider a
fall, muscle contraction injury or other trauma. Begin indicated
resuscitation and monitoring through the actual pathway.

47
00:09:06,168 --> 00:09:19,632
Small contact marks can conceal major deep-tissue injury. Reassess
limb perfusion and neurological function and report the
voltage/current history if known without pretending it quantifies the
full injury.

48
00:09:19,632 --> 00:09:32,616
Do not run fluid wide open simply to flush kidneys or assume every
exposure needs the same monitoring duration. Use actual hypoperfusion,
risk and the clinical/receiving pathway.

49
00:09:32,616 --> 00:09:44,208
Use the appropriate cool-water method to limit local thermal injury
and provide pain relief. Ice or excessive cold can worsen tissue
injury and whole-patient heat loss.

50
00:09:44,208 --> 00:09:54,576
Ask what effective cooling already occurred. The required total
includes that time; do not restart a full course simply because the
ambulance has arrived.

51
00:09:54,576 --> 00:10:05,880
Protect the rest of the patient with insulation and an appropriate
environment. Large areas, children and frail adults require particular
attention to systemic chilling.

52
00:10:05,880 --> 00:10:16,296
After suitable cooling and decontamination, change to the appropriate
covering and warming plan. Ongoing wet exposure is not automatically
better care.

53
00:10:16,296 --> 00:10:28,800
Current H10 says fifteen minutes of cool-water thermal-burn cooling.
The saved assigned provincial guide says fifteen to twenty minutes.
Both count effective prior bystander cooling.

54
00:10:28,800 --> 00:10:36,984
Keep the sources labelled instead of pretending the numbers are
identical or combining them into a newly invented mandatory duration.

55
00:10:36,984 --> 00:10:49,296
A patient with an immediate life threat needs the necessary treatment
and conveyance. Do not delay solely to remain at running water; use
the appropriate alternative while protecting the patient.

56
00:10:49,296 --> 00:11:01,512
For an actual school test, confirm the required revision. This lesson
teaches the shared clinical purpose and makes the discrepancy explicit
rather than supplying a false single official answer.

57
00:11:01,512 --> 00:11:14,592
After cooling and needed decontamination, cover with the appropriate
sterile nonstick or dry material and keep the patient warm. Do not
routinely break blisters or apply household creams.

58
00:11:14,592 --> 00:11:27,192
Assess pain even when a full-thickness centre is insensate. Use
authorized analgesia and gentle handling, then repeat the same pain
assessment and watch for adverse effects.

59
00:11:27,192 --> 00:11:39,792
Circumferential deep injury and swelling may threaten distal
circulation or chest expansion. Remove constricting jewellery when
safe and reassess neurovascular and breathing findings.

60
00:11:39,792 --> 00:11:52,368
Escharotomy is a distinct specialist intervention, not cutting taught
as an independent PCP response. Recognize the concern, communicate it
and expedite appropriate definitive care.

61
00:11:52,368 --> 00:12:03,096
Early hypotension in a burned patient requires a search for bleeding,
toxic effects and other causes. Do not assume the visible burn
explains every shock mechanism.

62
00:12:03,096 --> 00:12:15,984
A burn formula estimates a starting resuscitation plan over hours. Its
clock begins at injury and its volumes need adjustment; it is not an
amount to rapidly finish during a short ambulance trip.

63
00:12:15,984 --> 00:12:30,936
The textbook range, current adult burn-centre guidance and BCEHS
license-specific instructions are different sources. Do not convert a
CCP schedule or an adult formula into an independent pediatric or PCP
bolus.

64
00:12:30,936 --> 00:12:47,592
Give only indicated care under the applicable pathway and reassess
actual delivery and response. More edema, new respiratory difficulty
or persistent poor perfusion means review the plan, not automatically
finish a calculated maximum.

65
00:12:47,592 --> 00:13:00,144
Use the actual regional triage pathway. Current H10 includes large
partial or full-thickness burns, smaller major thresholds in older
adults and full-thickness injury above five percent.

66
00:13:00,144 --> 00:13:13,152
Critical locations and electrical, chemical or inhalation injury may
require specialist care even when the skin area is small. A hand or
airway problem is not made minor by a low percentage.

67
00:13:13,152 --> 00:13:25,656
Associated trauma and the changing patient also influence destination
and urgency. Do not use an older textbook major/minor table as if it
were the current local transfer rule.

68
00:13:25,656 --> 00:13:38,400
Communicate the injury time, mechanism, airway concern, area/depth
estimate, cooling already delivered and response. A useful pre-alert
prepares care before arrival.

69
00:13:38,400 --> 00:13:51,384
Hypothermia means a core temperature below thirty-five degrees
Celsius. Peripheral or field measurements may not represent core
temperature accurately, so consider exposure and the clinical picture.

70
00:13:51,384 --> 00:14:05,592
The teaching pattern is mild around thirty-two to thirty-five,
moderate around twenty-eight to thirty-two and severe below twenty-
eight. Do not treat boundary numbers as a substitute for actual
physiology or a measured core temperature.

71
00:14:05,592 --> 00:14:19,104
Shivering may diminish as the patient deteriorates; confusion, reduced
responsiveness, slow breathing or bradycardia can be important.
Absence of shivering is not proof that the patient is warm.

72
00:14:19,104 --> 00:14:30,216
Consider an older person in a cold home, a wet trauma patient or
someone with metabolic illness, not only a mountain rescue. Continue
to look for the cause and concurrent injury.

73
00:14:30,216 --> 00:14:42,024
Handle the hypothermic patient gently, prevent further exposure and
replace wet coverings appropriately. Insulate beneath and above and
use the treatment environment effectively.

74
00:14:42,024 --> 00:14:55,080
Use the approved warming method without placing a hot pack directly on
vulnerable skin. Continue airway, breathing, circulation and indicated
glucose assessment rather than focusing only on a thermometer.

75
00:14:55,080 --> 00:15:08,832
The assigned provincial snapshot says no active rewarming below
twenty-eight, while current I01 describes passive and active measures
differently. Keep that difference visible rather than creating a
blended rule.

76
00:15:08,832 --> 00:15:21,312
For patient care, confirm the applicable operational plan and seek
clinical support where needed. A historical classroom temperature
instruction is not automatically the whole current treatment pathway.

77
00:15:21,312 --> 00:15:34,680
In severe hypothermia, breathing and pulses may be very slow and
difficult to detect. A hasty check can miss signs of life, but an
unbounded delay is not an assessment strategy.

78
00:15:34,680 --> 00:15:48,264
I01 and the saved provincial page use thirty to forty-five seconds,
while the current N02 hypothermia branch uses sixty. These are source-
specific instructions, not numbers to average.

79
00:15:48,264 --> 00:16:02,760
The special pathway also changes shock and destination planning and
may involve advanced rewarming resources. Use the actual arrest
guideline and clinical consultation rather than inventing an all-
purpose cold arrest protocol.

80
00:16:02,760 --> 00:16:15,840
The limited-shock snapshot is not a rule for every cold-feeling trauma
patient or ordinary normothermic arrest. Later cardiac-resuscitation
teaching covers the complete applicable team sequence.

81
00:16:15,840 --> 00:16:26,856
A seventy-six-year-old in a very hot apartment is confused, weak and
breathing rapidly. The skin is sweaty. This does not exclude serious
heat illness.

82
00:16:26,856 --> 00:16:35,184
Do not require dry skin or a peripheral device reading above forty
before acting on a concerning environmental and neurological pattern.

83
00:16:35,184 --> 00:16:49,968
Assess airway, breathing, circulation, glucose when indicated and
possible concurrent illness. Heat illness can coexist with infection,
stroke, medicines or other causes of altered consciousness.

84
00:16:49,968 --> 00:17:01,800
Move to an appropriate cooler treatment environment and start
effective active cooling while arranging the care plan. Do not simply
give water and leave a confused person in the same room.

85
00:17:01,800 --> 00:17:12,600
Environmental heat illness occurs when heat accumulation exceeds
effective heat loss. It is not the same mechanism as an infection-
related change in the temperature set point.

86
00:17:12,600 --> 00:17:25,512
Significant neurological dysfunction is especially concerning for heat
stroke. A person may be sweating and still critically ill; skin
appearance alone does not separate every exhaustion and stroke case.

87
00:17:25,512 --> 00:17:37,536
Do not use antipyretics such as acetaminophen or aspirin to treat
environmental heat stroke. The key intervention is rapid effective
cooling with supportive care and appropriate conveyance.

88
00:17:37,536 --> 00:17:49,440
Investigate competing causes without withholding cooling from an
appropriately suspected heat emergency. Repeated neurological and
physiological assessment tests whether the plan is working.

89
00:17:49,440 --> 00:18:02,136
Begin the best available approved active-cooling method promptly and
continue during conveyance when feasible. A cooler room or ambulance
alone may be inadequate for a critically heat-injured patient.

90
00:18:02,136 --> 00:18:14,328
The assigned page includes wetting and convective airflow methods. Use
an effective patient-specific plan; fans alone in very hot air are not
a reliable heat-stroke treatment.

91
00:18:14,328 --> 00:18:28,224
Fluids address indicated dehydration or hypoperfusion and require
reassessment. Oral fluid is unsuitable when consciousness or
swallowing is unsafe and is not an effective primary cooling method.

92
00:18:28,224 --> 00:18:41,616
The snapshot ten-minute pre-conveyance wording must not postpone a
necessary rescue or treatment of another immediate threat. Coordinate
cooling, safe transfer, monitoring and early communication.

93
00:18:41,616 --> 00:18:54,816
The BCEHS guide below includes all eight named identifiers, not only
the familiar tourniquet page. It connects recognition, intervention,
reassessment and the license-level boundary.

94
00:18:54,816 --> 00:19:07,344
The provincial guide includes the separated-part page, Sager
examination sequence and image-only spinal diagram. A short topic
label must not hide a listed reading requirement.

95
00:19:07,344 --> 00:19:20,880
Nancy supplies the main-exam mechanism reading. It complements, rather
than replaces, the named school books. The other Week Four textbook
rows remain separate production work until their paired guides are
added.

96
00:19:20,880 --> 00:19:34,416
For review, explain one original patient through the whole sequence
and name an important source conflict. Memorizing a guideline code
without understanding the patient does not meet the learning purpose.

97
00:19:34,416 --> 00:19:46,080
At the burn station, identify the source, primary threats, included
area and method. Explain what cooling already occurred and how you
will avoid whole-patient chilling.

98
00:19:46,080 --> 00:19:57,384
At the cold station, demonstrate gentle movement and insulation, then
identify the actual rewarming and arrest sources without merging their
differing instructions.

99
00:19:57,384 --> 00:20:11,016
At the heat station, recognize neurological concern even with
sweating, initiate effective cooling and reassess the patient. Explain
why an antipyretic or a bottle of water alone is not the treatment.

100
00:20:11,016 --> 00:20:25,368
For each handover, give exposure and time, actual findings,
interventions already delivered, response and what remains urgent.
Keep estimates and suspected diagnoses clearly labelled.

101
00:20:25,368 --> 00:21:02,760
Read the four D02 hemorrhage classes as illustrative patterns, not
measured blood loss. Class I is up to fifteen percent with little rate
change and preserved pressure. Class II is fifteen to thirty percent:
pulse around one hundred to one hundred twenty, breathing around
twenty to twenty-four and a narrowing pulse pressure may precede a
systolic fall. These are source teaching bands, not permission to wait
for a percentage, a particular pulse or hypotension before controlling
bleeding. Age, medicines, pain and injury can alter the expected
pattern.

102
00:21:02,760 --> 00:21:48,024
Class III describes thirty to forty percent with hypotension and
mental-status change; Class IV exceeds forty percent, with severe
circulatory/mental depression and very low urine output. D02 flags
systolic pressure below ninety or a fall of twenty-to-thirty percent
from presentation as concerning. Its advanced column discusses blood
components, calcium support and targeted anticoagulant reversal, not
new PCP prescribing. The shared H01/D02 response is definitive
bleeding control, appropriate destination and limiting hypothermia,
acidosis and coagulopathy; a better pressure number is not replacement
of lost blood.

103
00:21:48,024 --> 00:22:32,640
The actual PR03 placement reference is five-to-eight centimetres above
the injury, or as high on the limb as possible, clear of joints and
directly on skin. Its sequence uses a direct-pressure attempt followed
by indicated device control, not repeated ineffective pressure while
catastrophic bleeding continues. Remove slack, tighten the actual
windlass device until bleeding stops, lock and record time, then
reassess after movement. The two-hour statement triggers planning and
consultation for delay, not scheduled loosening or automatic removal.
Crush-related use is a separate consultation consideration, not
routine prophylactic use for every trapped limb.

104
00:22:32,640 --> 00:23:14,376
In the PR02 T-POD example, expose the skin, position the belt at the
greater trochanters, trim it to leave its specified fifteen-to-twenty-
centimetre anterior gap, attach the tension system and draw it evenly
to circumferential compression. Record application and any required or
accidental release, maintain the closure and reassess. This is the
named T-POD design, not an instruction to cut every commercial binder
or a KED. Pelvic and isolated neck-of-femur injuries are not
interchangeable; use the actual indication, device and trained
movement method without repeated pelvic rocking.

105
00:23:14,376 --> 00:24:05,448
The H05 body defines full SMR as a collar, foam rolls without head
tape and a clamshell through transport; it reserves clamshell
conveyance for multi-trauma or new neurological impairment. The
separate adult resource distinguishes simple and full approaches, not
one board package for everyone. Assess cervical and thoracolumbar
concerns separately, repeat motor/sensory findings and adapt
protection to airway, breathing and positioning needs. The body states
a pressure-above-one-hundred-twenty target with clear neurological
deficit; the PCP bullet is broader, referring to suspected or
confirmed spinal cord injury. Preserve both wordings and seek clinical
support for competing hemorrhage, rather than silently narrowing the
PCP text or averaging targets.

106
00:24:05,448 --> 00:24:46,728
I01 describes cold diuresis and ataxia in milder illness, then reduced
shivering, slower physiology and declining awareness with worsening
hypothermia. Afterdrop means core temperature can continue to fall
during rescue as cold peripheral blood returns centrally. J or Osborn
waves can occur after the QRS, but are not a stand-alone temperature
measurement or a specific diagnosis. Keep movement gentle, prevent
further heat loss and check concurrent causes including glucose,
illness, trauma or intoxication. A stopped shiver or a warmer skin
surface does not by itself prove core recovery.

107
00:24:46,728 --> 00:25:35,256
The I01 PCP reference considers warmed saline at thirty-seven to
forty-two degrees Celsius for hypotension, thirty millilitres per
kilogram with a two-litre maximum when available. That is its
condition-specific source line, not routine fluid for every cold
person, an exception to child cannulation limits or a way to combine
every D03/BCPEG bolus figure. Assess breathing and perfusion
throughout. Its arrest section considers an extracorporeal-rewarming
destination within ninety minutes and recommends consultation when
options are unclear. Use actual access, transport and special-arrest
planning; do not infer that an unverified local service or a fixed
travel time guarantees acceptance.

108
00:25:35,256 --> 00:26:20,472
The January 2026 PR21 picture contrasts a BD twelve-gauge three-inch
nonfenestrated catheter with a Turkel twelve-gauge three-and-a-half-
inch fenestrated system. Turkel has a contact indicator and a stopcock
initially closed; the actual opening step matters. The BD example does
not require adding that stopcock. Control needle advancement at
pleural entry, stabilize while withdrawing the sharp and advancing the
flexible catheter, dispose of the sharp and reassess ventilation and
hemodynamics. An air sound alone is not success. The source device
pictures and adult sizing row are different references, not proof that
every supplied adult device has one length.

109
00:26:20,472 --> 00:27:08,976
The same dated PR21 table lists ages zero-to-four: twenty-two gauge,
twenty-five millimetres; five-to-seventeen: eighteen gauge, forty-
eight millimetres; eighteen-plus: twelve gauge, seventy-six
millimetres. Treat these as source examples for authorized supervised
teaching, not a universal child-device instruction or PCP
authorization. The precautions include body habitus, term-pregnancy
anatomy and mainstem intubation as a mimic of unilateral findings.
Incorrect diagnosis or landmarks can cause a pneumothorax or other
injury; blockage, kinking, displacement, bleeding and infection can
follow. No absolute contraindication in a true emergency does not mean
no indication is needed.

110
00:27:08,976 --> 00:28:01,968
Use the shock-drug interaction tables to obtain a real medication
history, not to invent a new contraindication. The salbutamol table
lists other sympathomimetics, epinephrine, MAO inhibitors, tricyclic
antidepressants and beta blockers; the norepinephrine table also
highlights MAO inhibitors, tricyclics and halogenated anesthetics.
Their practical questions concern altered effect, rhythm, pressure and
monitoring. A listed interaction is not a reason to replace indicated
IM epinephrine with salbutamol, or to delay lifesaving anaphylaxis
care. Verify the actual product and authorized plan. In a patient
already receiving an advanced infusion, communicate the medicine,
concentration, route, recent change, perfusion and line findings
rather than taking over its prescription.

111
00:28:01,968 --> 00:28:51,936
Reconcile generic name, brand, formulation and indication separately.
The skin table pairs hydrocortisone cream with Cortef, but the checked
Canadian Cortef record is an oral tablet, not that cream. Its
cantharidin row says Yanth and warts; the checked U.S. YCANTH label is
a clinician-applied product for molluscum contagiosum in people aged
two or older, not a universal wart instruction or proof of Canadian
availability. Retapamulin is another topical antibacterial for
selected impetigo treatment, while sodium sulfacetamide is a different
antibacterial skin preparation with its own allergy/product
precautions. These examples explain why one class or trade name cannot
choose the route, lesion, age or treatment schedule.

112
00:28:51,936 --> 00:29:39,192
The literal final pages include summaries and key terms, and those
shortcuts also need interpretation. Cardiac output is volume per
minute, whereas stroke volume is per beat; the source definition that
joins them is inconsistent. A dysrhythmia can be regular as well as
irregular. Atrial fibrillation does not guarantee a rapid ventricular
response. PEA means organized electrical activity without a clinically
detectable pulse; it is not established just by saying that every
muscle cell fails to respond. A vesicant can injure tissue outside an
IV context too, as the cantharidin example shows. Use the clinical
concept, not a mistaken one-line definition or a copied chapter-
question answer.

113
00:29:39,192 --> 00:30:26,352
Read the actual Parkland image as a hospital calculation, not a new
ambulance order. Its first-day lactated-Ringer estimate is four
millilitres times body weight in kilograms times the whole-number
percent of included TBSA. Thirty percent is entered as thirty, not
zero point three. Half of that estimated volume belongs to the first
eight hours from injury and half to the following sixteen hours. The
source also gives an adult urine-output benchmark above zero point
five millilitres per kilogram per hour. That does not authorize
catheterization or make urine output the only perfusion endpoint.
Prior fluids, elapsed time, associated hemorrhage and clinical
response must be reconciled by the responsible team.

114
00:30:26,352 --> 00:31:11,064
Original paper example: seventy kilograms with thirty percent included
TBSA gives four times seventy times thirty, or eight thousand four
hundred millilitres estimated over twenty-four hours. Half is four
thousand two hundred. If the exercise starts at the injury time with
no previous fluid, the two arithmetic rates are five hundred twenty-
five millilitres per hour over the first eight hours and two hundred
sixty-two point five over the next sixteen. The unequal rates follow
unequal time blocks; do not divide the total by twenty-four and call
that the first-phase rate. A delayed arrival changes the time
accounting, not permission to force a catch-up volume or deliver the
whole total as a bolus.

115
00:31:11,064 --> 00:31:54,720
Keep source versions separate. The school-copy image teaches the four-
millilitre Parkland model. The published 2024 American Burn
Association guideline recommends an initial two-millilitre-per-
kilogram-per-percent approach to reduce resuscitation volumes in its
defined adult group with burns of at least twenty percent TBSA. That
is a specialist burn-resuscitation comparison, not proof that the
school image was changed, a universal child formula or a replacement
BC PCP order. Both under-resuscitation and excessive fluid can harm.
State the model requested in an educational calculation, then use the
actual local protocol, consultation and titrated clinical plan in
practice.

116
00:31:54,720 --> 00:32:49,464
Interpret the actual oxygen-device table by interface and response,
not a fixed oxygen-per-litre rule. Its examples list a nasal cannula
at one-to-six litres per minute with an approximate twenty-four-to-
forty-four percent, a simple mask at six-to-ten with twenty-eight-to-
fifty percent, and a reservoir non-rebreather at ten-to-fifteen with
sixty-to-eighty percent. These are textbook estimates, not measured
guarantees or one setting for every patient. Reservoir inflation, mask
seal, inspiratory demand and device design matter. High-flow nasal
systems, CPAP and a ventilator are distinct technologies. A bag-mask
needs an appropriate gas supply, reservoir, seal and effective
breaths; fifteen litres does not automatically establish one hundred
percent inspired oxygen or adequate ventilation.

117
00:32:49,464 --> 00:33:35,256
The actual heart-listening image maps sound windows, not the
anatomical valve positions. In the usual adult surface map, aortic is
at the second right intercostal space near the sternum, pulmonic at
the second left, Erb's point at the third left, tricuspid near the
lower left sternal border and mitral near the fifth left intercostal
space at the midclavicular line. Compare sounds systematically with
the clinical context; body habitus and anatomy can alter access. S1
mainly accompanies atrioventricular-valve closure and S2 semilunar-
valve closure. These listening points do not replace rhythm, breathing
and perfusion assessment or prove a valve diagnosis from one sound.

118
00:33:35,256 --> 00:33:47,088
You should now distinguish cooling a thermal injury from warming a
cold patient and rapidly cooling environmental heat illness. The same
intervention can help one problem and harm another.

119
00:33:47,088 --> 00:33:58,368
Estimate burn depth and area with the correct age and reference,
recognize inhalation or electrical/chemical risk and avoid formula-
driven over-resuscitation.

120
00:33:58,368 --> 00:34:11,136
Use the three paired guides to retrieve mechanisms and all assigned
protocol pages. Keep source versions and clinical authority visible
rather than inventing a single blended official rule.

121
00:34:11,136 --> 00:34:23,520
Complete the original checks aloud, then rehearse the station and
handover with an appropriate partner or instructor. Reading completion
is preparation for performance, not proof of a passed skill.
